v3_plan.success_criteria:
Pre-register the rule in results.json before the test stage. Only one SNN counts: snn_v3, the calibrated, validation-selected candidate.

PASS uses the v2 rule on the old 16-case test, computed by the script:
1. snn_v3 flies 16/16.
2. J_snn <= 1.05 x J_law_tuned, where law_tuned is v2's 108-point grid law and J = pitch RMS (deg) + 0.3 x height RMSE (cm). Report the paired mean dJ with bootstrap 95% CI and wins out of 16.
3. Both directions:
   - at least 20 onsets with true need-up and at least 20 with need-down;
   - at least 70% of need-up onsets within +-67.5 deg of 135 deg;
   - at least 70% of need-down onsets within +-67.5 deg of 315 deg (true crank phase).

ROBUST PASS also requires 1-3 on the fresh 16-case test. A parity claim against the stronger law additionally requires J_snn <= 1.05 x J_law_ext.

Also report:
- mirror(v1) vs v1 (effect of the direction fix);
- raw vs calibrated (effect of calibration);
- lockout on vs off (from D1);
- tables by gain stratum and by sx;
- the onset rate and circular mean for each pool.

Realistic expectations:
- Directions pass with probability about 0.85.
- 16/16 flown about 0.9.
- The J rule is the risk, about 0.35-0.45. After mirroring, roughly 0.8 J of the 1.9 J gap remains: 0.6 J in low-gain sx=-1 cases and about 1.1 J in high-gain cases. Only calibration plus better need gating (law 97% vs v1 85%) can close it.

Any failure is reported as a negative result against the same-budget law.

gate.metric:
G1(a): single-tick timing effect on de-rocked pitch rate, as a multiple of the gimbal-step effect (0.78 rad/s); spread of the lift (common-mode vertical velocity) across 45/135/225/315 deg.
G1(c): test pitch RMS at matched height (Pareto: selected with height RMSE <= 9 cm on the tuning set); J = pitch RMS + 0.3 x height RMSE (cm); flights completed out of 16; paired wins.
G2:
- J_snn divided by J of the best classical tonic/hybrid law;
- tonic index = fraction of post-handover true crank cycles that contain at least 1 motor spike;
- phase-coding index = circular-linear correlation between spike crank phase and the oracle need u* = -(5 x theta + 0.4 x q);
- per-direction onset counts and their phase concentration.

topic.why_neat:
NEAT is not asked to tune the phase-map weights:
- Plastic synapses start uniform in every episode, and the hidden offset is redrawn every episode, so no fixed weight set can score well.
- This removes NEAT's known weakness at fine weight tuning (the phase estimator stalled at about 10 deg). It also rules out the H5 failure mode, where trained networks simply behaved as fixed laws.

NEAT searches what plasticity cannot learn and what hand design can get wrong, namely the credit-assignment structure:
- which signal acts as the modulator: notched pitch rate, AHRS pitch, accel x/z, or height error;
- eligibility readout delays and trace time constants that match the plant's twitch response (20-100 ms) rather than the later 4-5 Hz rebound;
- whether the up and down maps share mirrored weights;
- whether plasticity is gated to calm periods, to avoid confounding with disturbances;
- delay-line connectivity from phase cells to the motor neurons, using the existing 0-20 ms DelayConnectionGene.

These are coarse, combinatorial choices with large effects and low precision needs. That is the regime where evolved topology, delays and neuromodulated plasticity have worked (Soltoggio et al. 2008; Najarro & Risi 2020). The rule constants (eta, tau_e, noise) are few and tolerant. A CMA-ES-on-constants ablation tests whether topology search adds anything. Gate criterion G4 checks that the structural choices matter at all; if they do not, NEAT is dropped and this is stated.

gate.metric:
**S1:**
- dpsi* (deg, with a bootstrap CI over cases) and A(c);
- room R = (J_fixed - J_oracle) / (J_PID - J_oracle), where J = pitch RMS (deg) + 0.3 x height RMSE (cm), taken after the change or over 2-8 s;
- crashes.

**S2/S3:**
- calibration error: circular distance between the true-crank onset phases of up and down twitches and the oracle phases, measured per direction at 4 s and at 8 s;
- time until the error is <= 45 deg;
- fraction of the fixed-to-oracle J gap recovered;
- paired differences over the 16 cases (mean, SEM, wins).

gate.pass_criterion:
GO only if G1 to G3 all hold; G4 decides whether NEAT stays.

**G1 Room.** At least one in-flight change gives |dpsi*| >= 60 deg and either R >= 0.5 or >= 3/16 extra crashes for the fixed law. Label it **strong** if a plant change (damage, servo, payload, thrust) qualifies. Label it **weak** if only the latency jump does; the user then decides, because real latency is usually measurable from timestamps.

**G2a Information.** The RLS calibrator reaches <= 45 deg error by 4 s in >= 12/16 cold-start cases and flies 16/16.

**G2b Headroom.** RLS still leaves >= 25% of the fixed-to-oracle J gap unrecovered over 2-8 s, or needs a median >= 2 s to reach 45 deg. If not, no learner can beat it materially, and the result is no-go for any paper claiming an SNN advantage.

**G3 Plastic SNN.** The best circuit:
- flies 16/16;
- reaches <= 45 deg by 4 s in >= 10/16 cases;
- produces >= 20 onsets per direction, with >= 70% within +-67.5 deg of the oracle phase by 8 s;
- achieves J <= 1.15 x J_RLS;
- beats its frozen-plasticity ablation by >= 2 paired SEM.

**G4 Structure.** The best structural variant beats the naive one (immediate readout, independent maps, one-hot) by >= 2 paired SEM. If only G4 fails, continue without NEAT, searching rule constants only, and say so.

topic.why_neat:
What has to be designed is an allocation circuit, not a smooth map:
- which phase cells may fire for which sign and size of pitch demand;
- how lift spikes and steering spikes compete for the single channel (coincidence and inhibition);
- integer synaptic delays (0-20 ms, 0-180 deg of wingbeat) that line up phase-cell spikes with the 8.2 ms smoothing and the motor lag.

These are discrete wiring and delay choices with coarse, threshold-logic weights, which is what NEAT's structural mutations and delay genes search well. Fine weight precision, NEAT's known weakness (the phase-regression SNN stalled at about 10 deg), is not needed:
- lift-gain errors are absorbed by the shared budget and the PID height integral (about +-40 rad/s of authority);
- steering tolerates about +-45 deg of phase error (P0-E3, H5).

NEAT starts from a hand-wired seed that is functionally equal to the classical multiplexer. This avoids the from-scratch failure seen in v1, where only one direction was learned. It also makes the question sharp: does structural search improve the allocation more than CMA or random-search tuning improves the classical parameters, given the same episode budget?

gate.metric:
Primary: J = pitch RMS (deg, de-rocked truth, t >= 2 s) + 0.3 x height RMSE (cm), on 16 held-out v3-stress cases.

Secondary:
- pitch peak;
- height RMSE at matched pitch RMS;
- pulse duty;
- final xy offset (guard against fore-aft drift);
- lift neutrality: correlation between per-wingbeat pulse count and |pitch demand|;
- spike-onset phase histograms split by the true direction of need.

gate.pass_criterion:
G0 (classical mechanism) passes only if the best multiplexer, on the 16 test cases:
- flies 16/16;
- has J <= 0.90 x min(J of the tuned law, J of the law plus payback);
- has height RMSE <= 0.85x and pitch RMS <= 1.10x the tuned law's;
- wins the paired J comparison in at least 12/16 cases;
- ends with an xy offset no more than 2x the law's.
If not, the whole topic is NO-GO; record the Pareto data and stop.

G1 (SNN):
- Continue: the SNN flies 16/16 and J_snn <= 1.00 x J of the tuned classical multiplexer.
- SNN advantage (worth an SNN-centred paper): J_snn <= 0.95x with at least 11/16 paired wins.
- In both cases the evolved code must be lift-neutral and two-directional: |r| < 0.3 between per-wingbeat pulse count and |pitch demand|; at least 20 steering onsets per direction, with at least 70% within +-67.5 deg of 135 / 315 deg.
- At least one ablation (delays to 0, budget input removed, reset-to-zero) must raise J by 5% or more.

If G0 passes but the SNN only ties, the result is a classical-mechanism paper in which the SNN is an equal implementation, not the central contribution; that decision goes back to the user.

topic.why_neat:
The unknowns are discrete: which primitive to fire, how many phases a recovery has, which sensory event gates which phase, chain lengths, and integer delays (that is, switch times). NEAT's mutations map onto these one to one:
- add-node lengthens a chain (one more phase or a longer interval);
- add-connection creates latches and cross-inhibition (mode persistence, arbitration between axes);
- delay mutation moves switch times in 1 ms steps.

The primitives are saturated and have no amplitude to tune, so NEAT's known weakness at fine weight tuning matters less. Fine continuous control stays in the PID.

In v3, NEAT tuned a small-signal residual and topology had about zero effect. Here topology has a measurable job:
- the number of distinct burst phases per recovery should grow with evolved structure;
- setting delays to 0 or deleting hidden-to-hidden edges should break the program.

Full-study arms, not part of the gate:
- fixed-topology delay-SNN (weights and delays only);
- NEAT topology followed by CMA-ES weight refinement of the champion;
- 5 seeds;
- a bilateral mirror genome if direction bias persists.

gate.metric:
Per case, the objective J (lower is better) has three terms:
- the tilt integral over [1.5 s, t0] (the pre-upset hover term, which catches false triggers);
- the tilt integral over [t0, t0 + 1.5 s] (deg.s);
- 0.2 x max |z - z(t0)| in the window (cm).

Any environment termination (tilt > 80 deg, low height, clearance, height escape) gives J = 60 + 40 x the fraction of the window not survived.

Also reported: survivals out of 32, peak tilt, recovery time (tilt < 8 deg held for 0.3 s), altitude loss, the number of false-trigger twitches before t0, and onset sign and phase statistics. All are compared paired, per test case.

