fixes:
If the claim of timing carried by structure is kept, give the network a way to time 50-150 ms intervals; otherwise drop the claim. Options:
- raise MAX_DELAY to >= 150 ms, or add slow or adaptive-threshold units (tau 50-150 ms);
- override DelayGenome.mutate_add_node so the two new edges inherit the original delay (split as d1 + d2) and the new LIF unit reproduces the old edge's behaviour;
- seed hidden-to-hidden latch or chain edges in the initial genome;
- lower the per-edge delay mutation churn (about 55 Gaussian jumps of 3 ms per offspring over 184 edges).

Make B2 specific to sequencing:
- zero or shuffle delays only on hidden-to-hidden edges, keeping the input and phase-cell delays;
- add a sensory-clamp test that freezes the rate and attitude inputs after the first counter twitch; an internal program must still fire the brake on time;
- require an opposite-sign gimbal brake 50-150 ms after the counter twitch, instead of 'brake or motor twitch'.

Reduce noise:
- use >= 96-128 paired test cases (about 15-20 extra minutes);
- calibrate upset levels per axis in induced-rate units, since roll has only the gimbal;
- report survival curves together with continuous metrics (peak whole-body tilt, angular-momentum recovery time) at sub-critical levels;
- give every genome and every CEM candidate >= 8 shared cases.

topic.novelty_notes:
**Searched 2026-10-13. Close but different:**

- **Evolution combined with gradients.** This exists mostly as architecture search for classification:
  - evolving SNN time constants and surrogate functions (arXiv 2410.18580);
  - Lamarckian weight inheritance in EXAMM, for RNNs (EvoStar 2021);
  - arXiv 2606.20817 found Lamarckian and Baldwinian inheritance about equal for NEAT with plasticity.
- **Delays.** NEAT with delay genes exists (tau-NEAT, Caamano et al., Neural Processing Letters 2016). Gradient-learned delays exist too: DCLS (ICLR 2024), Meszaros, Knight and Nowotny (Frontiers in Computational Neuroscience 2024), and DelRec (arXiv 2509.24852). All of these are evaluated on classification, not control.
- **SNN control by gradients:**
  - surrogate gradients through a learned forward model, on imagined rollouts (Huebotter et al., arXiv 2509.05356);
  - SNN reinforcement learning with adaptive surrogate gradients and a privileged guiding policy on a real drone (Van den Berghe et al., NeurIPS 2025, arXiv 2510.24461).

  Both use continuous outputs and learned critics or models.
- **Spikes as control events:**
  - a closed-form rule where a neuron spikes only if that brings a linear system closer to its target (Agliati et al., arXiv 2507.16495): known linear plant, full state, no learning;
  - spike trains convolved with a fixed kernel to produce plant force (arXiv 1708.02603).
- **Flapping-wing SNNs:**
  - Clawson et al. 2017: adaptive SNN hover, in simulation;
  - arXiv 2605.19430: SNN on an ESP32 for a butterfly-like robot, trained by imitation learning, modulating a central pattern generator.

**Not found:** identified, phase-indexed event-response kernels of a non-differentiable plant used as a differentiable layer, to give per-spike hindsight credit on real rollouts, combined with NEAT evolving the discrete timing structure, for wingbeat-phase-locked one-sided twitch control.

**Expected reviewer objection.** The credit will be called a linearized model-based policy gradient, similar to SVG or ILC. The defence:
- the credit is applied per spike on real trajectories, with no imagined-rollout model to exploit;
- the division of labour with NEAT is measured, not assumed.

top.why:
Ranked first because it is the only proposal whose premise survives the verified headroom finding. Large upsets are a new disturbance class, allowed as a test scenario, in which the slow PID is structurally weak:
- 0.13 s attitude lag;
- D term saturating the 0.3 rad gimbal 35-115 ms into a 4-10 rad/s upset;
- range finder blind above 45.6 deg tilt.

Stage A is purely classical (about 1.2 h) and informative on its own: it maps whether nonlinear, multi-phase event logic has any leverage on this two-body plant, the open question in gates_report section 5.6. It is also the only topic where NEAT's structural search has a plausible job (sequencing and arbitration across axes, directions and double hits).

It is still weak (critics 3/3): as proposed, the network cannot hold 50-150 ms intervals, GO was set at parity, the lesion tests were confounded, and 32 survival-dominated cases are underpowered.

The plan below folds in the critics' fixes:
- primitive characterization;
- per-axis calibration;
- a base PID retuned on upsets;
- one-shot and gain-scheduled classical baselines;
- 96 test cases;
- slow/adaptive units and a delay-preserving add-node;
- matched non-spiking and fixed-topology arms;
- sequencing-specific probes;
- a strong GO bar.

Expected outcomes: Stage A NO-GO about 70%; Stage A passes but Stage B fails about 25%; SNN/NEAT-central GO about 3-5%.

gate_steps:
Step 1, primitive characterization (about 5 min).
Setup: 4 stress-like cases with the v3 pulses off and delay_steps=1. Use paired with/without runs, as in timing.run/measure.
Primitives, applied (i) in hover at t=2.4 s and (ii) 60 ms after a mid-level upset:
- a +-0.3 rad alpha gimbal twitch and a +-0.3 rad beta gimbal twitch, each with 20 ms dwell;
- a 3-tick motor-HIGH twitch in each of 8 true-crank 45-deg bins.
Log:
- base_link and thrust-unit de-rocked attitude/rate (gates.Oracle 'derocked' and 'thrust_derocked');
- whole-robot angular momentum (mujoco.mj_subtreeVel, then data.subtree_angmom of the free root body);
- vertical velocity.
Keep only primitives whose effect on thrust-unit or whole-body tilt is restoring within 100 ms. Record the wrong-way base_link excursion (critic estimate: 8-10 deg).

Step 5, Stage A test (about 10 min).
Cases: 96 held-out paired cases (3 direction classes covering 8 directions, 3 levels, 4 seeds; half with a second hit 80-200 ms later), on seeds disjoint from training.
Score J = pre-t0 tilt integral + tilt integral over [t0, t0+1.5 s] (deg.s) + 0.2 x max|z - z(t0)| from truth (cm). A crash scores 60 + 40 x the unsurvived fraction.
Also report:
- survivals;
- base_link and thrust-unit peak tilt;
- recovery time (tilt < 8 deg held for 0.3 s);
- altitude loss;
- false-trigger twitches before t0;
- PLL phase error against the true crank during recovery.
If A fails, optionally run the privileged per-case open-loop schedule bound (CEM 24 x 10 on 4 cases, about 17 min) to tell 'no headroom' from 'weak automaton'. Stop here unless A passes and the user approves Stage B.

Step 1, kernel identification (about 10 min).
Forced single 3-tick HIGH twitches on 8 balanced stress cases:
- onsets in the gust windows (2.6-3.2 s and 4.6-5.2 s) and in calm windows;
- one onset per true-crank 45-deg bin;
- each paired with a no-twitch run (timing.run/measure methodology) and run to t0 + 1.5 s.
Fit two kernels with a 2nd-order Fourier basis in phi, separately for PID margin < 40 and >= 40 rad/s:
- h_theta(phi, tau) on de-rocked pitch, tau <= 0.8 s;
- h_z(phi, tau) on true height, tau <= 1.5 s.
